  "query_preview": "..\nToday and tonight: High pressure will continue the warm, dry \nconditions for today. With an incoming cold front , clouds will\nsill increase through the day. Winds will be generally light \nand variable. Highs for the day will be in the 70s and low 80s. \nOvernight lows will be in the 40s and low 50s.\n\nFriday through Sunday: On Friday, a Low will slide south along \nthe coast line and push a cold front across the Inland \nNorthwest. It will bring mainly mountain showers, gusty winds,\nand colder temperatures. Moisture amounts with the system are \nnot too high. Precipitable water amounts are 0.50-0.75 of an \ninch, 120-140 percent of normal. Rain chances start over the \nCascades on Friday morning and spread east through the day. \nSaturday the upper low continues precipitation chances in the \nCascades and northern mountains and ID and into central WA. The \nchances decrease into Sunday. Behind the front, a long trough\nwill continue pushing cold air mass through the weekend. Snow \nlevels will start around 7-8kft on Friday, then 4.5-5.5kft \nSaturday morning and afternoon. Rain amounts expected to be little\nto none across the Basin and under a tenth for the mountains \nwith highest amounts along the Cascade Crest. The higher\nelevations of the mountains should get some light snow \naccumulation. Amounts of 0.5 to 2 inches are expected, but \nlocally higher amounts are possible. Hikers and campers at \nthese elevations and higher should be prepared for light snow \nand freezing temperatures. \n\nWinds will be main impact for most of the region as the\npressure gradient tightens across the Basin with front passage.\nHighest winds are expected Friday afternoon and evening,from the\nwest-southwest at 20-30 mph with gusts of 35 to 45 mph. Areas \nexpected to the see strongest winds include the lower lee of the\nCascades across the basin toward the Palouse/Spokane/CdA area. \nThere is a 60-80% probability of 40 mph or greater. Possible \nimpacts are blowing dust across the Columbia Basin where soils \nremain dry. Gusts will also create difficult travel conditions, \nespecially for high profile vehicles, and could blow around \nunsecured objects. Isolated power outages are also possible.\nWinds turn more northerly Saturday into Sunday. Saturday \nbreezy/gusty winds continue across the Okanogan Valley, \nWaterville Plateau, and Columbia Basin. Sunday wit breezy \nconditions will be broader again, with gusts of 30 to 35 mph and\nthe another chance for blowing dust.\n\nColder air mass will lead to temperatures drop through this \nweekend, with highs in the mid-60s to mid-70s Friday falling to \nthe mainly the 50s Saturday and Sunday. Lows will be in the 40s \nSaturday morning, then 20s and 30s Sunday and Monday morning. So\nfrost and freezing temperatures are in the forecast. \n\nMonday to Wednesday: The new week starts dry and continued cool\nwith a weak ridge over the region on Monday. Another wave moves\nin later Tuesday into Wednesday with shower chances across the \nmountains and eastern third of WA and ID. Ensemble guidance \nshows some disagreement on precip amounts for this period. For \nnow, 75 percent of ensemble clusters are going with light \nprecip. 25 percent are trending wetter. Highs will be more \nseasonable in the 50s to low 60s, with lows in the upper 20s to \n30s. /JDC",
